rev12 (VR R11 review fix, 2026-09-06). rev11 introduced Player_VrAttackVoice
with a sohHard parameter and then passed a literal 0 at both of its call sites,
so the HEAVY pair -- NA_SE_IT_SWORD_SWING_HARD and NA_SE_VO_LI_SWORD_L -- was
unreachable through it. Vanilla plays that pair on the THIRD hit of a combo
(func_80833A20's `unk_845 >= 3`), and the count that feeds it lives in
func_80837948, inside the authored attack machine rev5 retires for a covered
weapon. So VR had no combo escalation at all: every press and every arm swing
was the light pair, forever.

The count is kept in ONE place, Player_VrAttackCombo, which reproduces vanilla's
own arithmetic (reset when the chain is broken or when the previous attack was
already the third, then increment, heavy at >= 3) with "the animation differs"
replaced by "the chain window has expired" -- seven game ticks, the same window
the B stab already uses for its damage. It is advanced once per tick, consulted
by the two voiced sites, and RESET both when motion combat stops covering the
weapon and on a chop, which is what vanilla does too (the jump slash writes a
different meleeWeaponAnimation, so it breaks the chain).

The Z+A chop is deliberately NOT one of the voiced sites and does not pass
hard=1: vanilla's own func_8083BA90 ends with
Player_PlayVoiceSfx(NA_SE_VO_LI_SWORD_L) -- the heavy voice, already -- and it
runs for every press that produces a chop. Calling Player_VrAttackVoice there
would be a SECOND voice on one press, which is the thing rev11 was built to
stop. One press, one voice, at every site.

rev11 (VR R11 verdicts 3 and 5, 2026-09-06). Two of the user's 1.0.1.14 reports,
both created by rev10, and both about the SAME press:

  * "LINK NO LONGER MAKES THE YELL ON THE BUTTON ATTACK." rev10 let vanilla's
    Z-target jump slash run again for its leap, and func_8083BA90 ->
    func_80837948 writes this->meleeWeaponAnimation = PLAYER_MWA_JUMPSLASH_START
    (17, or 19 on a combo). NOTHING in a VR session writes it back, because every
    other writer lives inside the authored-attack machine rev5 retires for a
    covered weapon -- and func_80833A20, the setter rev8 routed the swing sound
    through, suppresses its VOICE for the whole FLIPSLASH_START..JUMPSLASH_FINISH
    range (16..19). So from the first jump attack of a session onward, every B
    press was a swish with no yell, for the rest of the session. A state setter
    is not a sound trigger: Player_VrAttackVoice reproduces vanilla's SELECTION
    of the light and heavy pair and drops its GATING, and it is called on the
    PRESS, so every B press yells exactly once. Two latent edges R10's review
    recorded go with it -- a press taken while meleeWeaponState was already 1 was
    silent, and the deferred yell's pending flag could survive a coverage drop.

  * "IN THE Z-TARGETING ATTACK (WHICH I DID HEAR YELLING), THE SWORD ISN'T
    MOVING, ONLY THE SWISH." Two predicates for one move. The seam started the
    chop on Player_VrZTargetActive -- a focusActor AND a converged reticle
    (targetCtx.unk_4B != 0) -- while the leap he heard gates on vanilla's
    Player_IsZTargeting, which is hostile lock-on OR friendly-or-PARALLEL. Hold Z
    with nothing lockable, or press A during the reticle's convergence, and
    vanilla leapt and yelled while the shell's envelope never started. The chop
    counter and its damage window are set AT VANILLA'S OWN SITE now, one line
    before func_8083BA90, so the envelope runs if and only if the leap does. That
    also retires rev10's deferred yell arbiter outright: vanilla yells for every
    press that produces a chop, so the chop's onset is the one branch that must
    stay silent, and gSohVRChopYells is 0 by construction.

rev9 (VR R9 part B review fix, 2026-09-05). rev8's shield stance shipped with
one gate missing: the stop it added inside Player_UpdateCommon, and the neutral
stick it reports out of Player_GetMovementSpeedAndYaw, both ran during blocking
cutscenes. That matters because the stance is not something the wearer opts out
of when a scene starts -- PLAYER_STATE1_SHIELDING is vanilla's own flag and can
still be set as the scene begins, and the left grip is held or released entirely
at his discretion -- so a cutscene that walks Link by script would have had its
motion zeroed every tick and never reached its mark. Both consumers now carry
!Player_InBlockingCsMode(play, this), which is the same exclusion the kinematic
branch beside them and the facing pin below them already had; the predicate
itself is unchanged, so the crouch (an eye height, not a motion) still follows
the grip wherever it is held.

rev8 (VR R9 part B, 2026-09-05). Three of the user's 1.0.1.12 rulings, and they
are all the same shape -- a mechanism this overlay withdrew, whose vanilla side
effects nobody replaced:

  * "THE LEFT GRIP SHIELD CROUCH LOWERS YOUR HEIGHT BUT NOW YOU CAN MOVE
    AROUND." Vanilla stops a shielding Link inside Player_Action_80843188
