------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A. STEERING -- the four-case table (donor Player_GetSteeringYaw,
   z_player.c:2072-2094), at EXACTLY two call sites,
   Player_ProcessControlStick and Player_GetMovementSpeedAndYaw. The other
   Camera_GetInputDirYaw sites in the file are not stick steering and are left
   alone.

  1. not VR / not first person / no head yaw yet -> vanilla.
  2. CRAWLING or GRABBING_DYNAPOLY -> actor.shape.rot.y. Two recorded donor
     bugs: head-relative steering in a crawlspace made the exit unreachable
     when the head was off-axis, and pushing a block wandered wherever the
     player gazed.
  3. LOCK-ON FRAMING ACTIVE -> actor.shape.rot.y, the body, which vanilla keeps
     aimed at the target.
  4. otherwise -> the head yaw the shell publishes.

  rev1 BUG, fixed here: rev1's case 3 was `focusActor != NULL`, which fires
  whenever anything is targetable -- but the donor's case 3 is `sVrLockOnActive`,
  i.e. Legaiaflame's Lock On, which ships DEFAULT OFF (DONOR-MAP 4d, spec
  scope "lock-on framing default-OFF"). rev1 therefore silently handed steering
  back to the body during ordinary Z-targeting, which is neither vanilla nor
  the donor. rev2 reproduces Player_VrLockOnBody exactly, gated on
  gSohVRLockOn (default 0), and LATCHES it once per player update so that
  steering (read in Player_ProcessControlStick, which runs BEFORE the action
  func) and the facing pin cannot disagree about the same tick -- that latch is
  the donor's own recorded scar (z_player.c:2043-2058).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
B. LINK'S BODY (donor Player_OverrideLimbDrawGameplayVRFirstPerson,
   z_player_lib.c:1514-1611), selected in Player_Draw AHEAD of the vanilla
   unk_6AD projected-head-position test (donor z_player.c:12761-12765) -- that
   test projects the head through the THIRD-PERSON matrix and does not apply.

  - HEAD and HAT always culled: the camera is inside Link's skull. rev1 had
    this and stopped there, which is why turning around showed the inside of a
    torso, backface-culled and stretched by the near plane. That is what the user
    photographed.
  - gSohVRHideBody (donor gVrHideBody, default 1) hides EVERY REMAINING LIMB
    EXCEPT THE TWO HANDS. Held items ride the hand matrices, so sword and
    shield stay visible for free -- that is a property of PostLimbDraw, not
    something this function arranges.
  - R3 SCOPE (honest): the donor pins the hand limbs to VR_GetHandMatrix (the
    Sense controller pose) when gVrMotionHands is on, and with motion hands OFF
    its gVrHideBody branch hides the hands too, because there they belong to
    arms that are not there. We are one round short of hand poses (R4, Q-VR3
    closed by the user: hands/motion combat ARE part of the minimum), so R3 keeps
    the two hand limbs drawn AT THEIR ANIMATED POSITIONS and hides everything
    else. The hook R4 needs is the marked block below: replace the two hand
    limbs' matrices with the controller pose and delete nothing else.

  - rev3 (VR R4) TAKES THAT HOOK. With gSohVRMotionHands on and the hand
    TRACKED, the limb's matrix is replaced outright with the controller pose
    the shell publishes (Matrix_Put, donor z_player_lib.c:1594), pos and rot
    zeroed so the base skeleton contributes nothing, and the SHOULDERS and
    FOREARMS hidden -- a floating hand attached to an arm that is not there is
    worse than no arm. HELD ITEMS RIDE THE HAND MATRICES FOR FREE: sword,
    shield, bow, hookshot, bottle, ocarina and sticks all draw on this same
    matrix in PostLimbDraw, so nothing item-specific appears here. Link's model
    scale is folded back in after the Put, which is what makes child and adult
    both work (the donor's VR_SetHandScale).

    WHICH CONTROLLER DRIVES WHICH LIMB: Link is left-handed, so his L_HAND is
    the SWORD hand and his R_HAND is the SHIELD hand. The player's RIGHT
    controller drives the sword hand by default (gSohVRLeftHanded 0 swaps it).
    Each controller therefore drives Link's OPPOSITE-side hand model, which is
    why the donor mirrors the mesh per hand -- and mirrors PER HAND rather than
    globally, because the reflection also mirrors held items' face designs: the
    sword survives that, the shield's crest reads upside-down. A mirrored mesh
    has reversed triangle winding, so G_EX_INVERT_CULLING is set for it and
    CLEARED ON THE NEXT LIMB (the donor's sVrHandInvertCulling latch) -- clearing
    it inline would clear it before the held item had drawn.

    FALLBACK, and it is the whole no-Sense story: gSohVRHandValid[] is the only
    test. No pair connected, tracking lost, or a hand out of view and that hand
    falls back to R3's ANIMATED position on the very next frame, with the
    shoulders and forearms coming back with it. Nothing else needs to know.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
D. MOTION COMBAT (VR R4, DONOR-MAP 8), and the honest shape of it.

  The donor drives damage from the PHYSICAL BLADE: its layer-0 solver stops the
  sword at surfaces, and VrSwing.cpp turns each damaging sim contact into a
  swept ColliderQuad poked 6 units inside the surface. That needs a collision
  mesh, and both of its sources for one are out of scope this round (the
  visual-mesh harvest is a renderer feature; the gVrPhysVisualMesh=0 path
  harvests the game's own collision mesh at the tick). So R4 does NOT reproduce
  the physical blade.

  What it does instead is smaller and, for this round, better: A DETECTED SWING
  PRESSES B FOR YOU. sUseHeldItem is the single static that means "use the held
  item this frame" -- it is what Player_ProcessItemButtons sets from the B
  press, and it is reset to 0 every frame immediately before actionFunc runs
  (z_player.c:12055). Setting it there on a swing edge means the ENTIRE authored
  attack path runs unchanged: the right animation for the state, combo chains,
  the stab lunge, jump-slash when airborne (func_8083BB20 reads sUseHeldItem
  itself), every enemy's own damage table and vulnerability set, the vanilla
  swept quads, the SFX and the voice. Nothing is re-implemented, so nothing can
  be re-implemented wrongly.

  This is the OPPOSITE of the donor's choice, which SUPPRESSES that whole state
  machine (func_8083BB20 -> return 0) precisely because it damages from the
  blade instead. We keep it, so we must not suppress it -- and the honest cost
  is stated plainly: the damage lands along LINK'S AUTHORED SWORD ARC, not where
  your hand actually is. A swing to the left still attacks whatever Link is
  facing. Recorded in docs/VR-R4-NOTES.md as the round's biggest parity gap.

  ONE SWING, ONE ATTACK, across a 90/120 Hz producer and a 20 Hz consumer: the
  shell bumps gSohVRSwingSeq once per rising edge into the HOT tier and never
  otherwise; this side keeps its own last-seen value and fires on a difference.
  A dropped frame cannot lose an edge and a slow tick cannot double one.

  GATING: melee only (Player_GetMeleeWeaponHeld). A swing while the bow is out
  must not fire the bow -- sUseHeldItem is the held item's button, not the
  sword's, and that is the one way this seam can misfire.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
E. THE ITEM TRIGGER (VR R4, DONOR-MAP 8 / HANDOFF-ITEM-TRIGGER.md).

  The donor's killer detail, ported whole because it is nearly free: the hand
  holding an item has its TRIGGER mirror that item's own N64 button as raw pad
  STATE, not as a press. Press nocks the bow, hold keeps it drawn, release
  looses it; ammo, magic, bottles and aim-and-throw all apply with nothing
  re-implemented, because the game is simply seeing its own button held.

  All this side does is publish WHICH button that is -- sItemButtons[
  heldItemButton], the same table Player_ProcessItemButtons reads -- into
  gSohVRItemTriggerMask. Overlay 0047 ORs it into the pad from padmgr while the
  trigger is down, and reserves that trigger from the generic binding table so
  one physical input cannot mean two things. Published as 0 whenever a MELEE
  weapon is held, because there the swing IS the attack and the trigger has
  nothing to do (the donor's VrCombat_MeleeCovered).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
C. DIRECT MOVEMENT (donor gVrBodyFollowsHead, default 1). Four surgical
   overrides plus the facing pin. Without these, Link's body keeps vanilla's
   camera-relative turn easing while the camera sits at his eyes -- which is
   exactly "third person with the camera in front of his face".

  1. func_8083C484 -> return 0 (donor :6693). No pivot brake: reversing the
     stick just moves the other way. The body does not turn with movement in
     VR, so there is nothing to pivot.
  2. func_8083DF68 (donor :7286): this->yaw = arg2 before Math_ScaledStepToS --
     velocity goes exactly where the stick points, never bent by turn easing.
  3. func_8083DFE0 (donor :7305): same for the air/jump variant, and no
     reversal brake.
  4. The KINEMATIC OVERRIDE in Player_UpdateCommon (donor :12106-12123), placed
     immediately before Actor_UpdateVelocityXZGravity so it overrides whatever
     the later-running action func eased in: while in a neutral locomotion
     action, grounded, and not in a blocking cutscene, velocity IS the stick.
     No acceleration ramp, no start-step latency, no stop-slide.
     Player_VrIsNeutralLocomotion whitelists exactly the donor's seven action
     functions; rolls, attacks, jumps and knockbacks keep their authored motion.
  5. The FACING PIN (donor :12340-12356), the LAST facing writer in the update
     loop, immediately after Player_UpdateShapeYaw: shape.rot.y = head yaw.
     Velocity is built from this->yaw, not shape.rot.y, so pinning never
     redirects movement -- it only turns the body to face where you look, which
     is what makes "turn around and you cannot see your own back" true.
     Skipped in cutscenes, on horse, on ladders, ledge-climb, hanging,
     crawling, dynapoly-grab, and lock-on -- every state where the game
     choreographs Link's facing.

