+/* SOH_IOS (0042 rev5, VR R7 verdict 5): IS VANILLA LOCK-ON ENGAGED RIGHT
+ * NOW? -- which is a different question from "should the world rotate
+ * toward the target", and conflating the two is what broke Z-targeting.
+ *
+ * the user, 2026-09-04: Z-targeting on the left trigger did not work -- no
+ * lock-on strafe, no side-hop, no backflip. The binding was never the
+ * problem: overlay 0047 has bound the left trigger to BTN_Z since R4 and
+ * the press reaches the game. What broke was everything Z is FOR.
+ *
+ * Vanilla's lock-on works by aiming Link's BODY at the target every tick
+ * (Player_UpdateShapeYaw), and then reading the stick in that body frame:
+ * forward closes, left and right circle, back-and-jump is the backflip,
+ * sideways-and-jump is the side-hop. R3's facing pin is the LAST writer of
+ * actor.shape.rot.y in the tick, so it overwrote that aim with the head's
+ * yaw on every single tick of every lock-on. The body never faced the
+ * target, so vanilla never produced a strafe, a hop or a flip -- and from
+ * the headset that reads exactly as "Z does nothing".
+ *
+ * The exclusion for it already existed and was gated on the WRONG thing.
+ * gSohVRLockOn was the comfort option "turn the world toward a Z-target",
+ * which shipped OFF by design (rotating the player's view for them is the
+ * classic sickness trigger, DONOR-MAP 4d) -- so with the shipped default
+ * the exclusion never fired and the pin ran through every lock-on.
+ *
+ * This predicate asks only whether vanilla's own lock-on branch will aim
+ * the body this frame, takes no CVar at all, and gates the FACING PIN and
+ * the steering frame -- so Z-targeting behaves as vanilla, always. rev6
+ * (VR R8 item 9) retires the comfort option and Player_VrLockOnBody with
+ * it; this one is not a preference and never was. */
+/* SOH_IOS (0042 rev11, VR R11 verdict 3): THE ATTACK VOICE, PLAYED
+ * DIRECTLY -- because a state setter is not a sound trigger.
