+/* SOH_IOS (overlay 0042 rev14, VR R13, Q-VR28): WHERE A PROJECTILE GOES.
+ *
+ * the user, on 1.0.1.16: "I can fire now, but it shoots way up HIGH and to
+ * the RIGHT (sword on right) / HIGH and to the LEFT (sword on left). It
+ * needs to follow where my aiming is."
+ *
+ * R12 predicted the mesh MIRROR: a rotation Euler-extracted from a
+ * reflected matrix is not the rotation of the un-reflected frame. THAT IS
+ * NOT THE CAUSE, and retiring it is half of this round.
+ * Matrix_MtxFToYXZRotS reads yaw = atan2(mf->xz, mf->zz) and pitch =
+ * atan2(-mf->yz, hypot(mf->xz, mf->zz)) -- the THIRD COLUMN and nothing
+ * else -- and Actor_SetProjectileSpeed flies the actor along exactly that
+ * column, while Matrix_Scale(-1, 1, 1) negates column ZERO. Vanilla's aim
+ * extraction is reflection-safe by construction; only the ROLL it also
+ * writes is corrupted, and roll is the arrow's cosmetic spin.
+ *
+ * The cause is the fixed authored correction that sits between the limb
+ * matrix and that extraction. Player_PostLimbDrawGameplay's L_HAND branch
+ * applies Matrix_RotateZYX(0x69E8, -0x5708, 0x458E) first, which carries the
+ * arrow's axis to (0.413, 0.787, 0.459) in the limb frame -- 42 degrees to
+ * the RIGHT of and 52 degrees ABOVE the limb's own +Z. It is authored for
+ * the ANIMATED bow-draw pose; in VR the limb matrix IS the controller, so
+ * those two numbers land on the controller. "High, and to the right." And
+ * the mirror does explain the FLIP after all, one layer down: the fixed
+ * correction mixes 0.413 of column ZERO into the composed third column, and
+ * the mirror negates column zero in right-handed play only.
+ *
+ * So: the direction is the held item's own forward axis carried through the
+ * pinned, calibrated hand pose as a VECTOR, and vanilla's fixed correction
+ * is not applied at all. The axis, the frame and two angular trims are
+ * console-tunable (`vr aim`, `vr set aimaxis|aimpitch|aimyaw|aimframe`)
+ * because a sign error here has to be correctable from a headset.
+ *
+ * Which hand: the one HOLDING the aimable item. PLAYER_MODELTYPE_RH_* is
+ * Link's off hand and _LH_* his sword hand, so the bow/slingshot and the
+ * hookshot aim from the off hand even though the arrow is nocked in the
+ * sword hand, and the boomerang aims from the sword hand. */
+static s32 Player_VrAimAimable(s32 sohModel) {
+    return (sohModel == PLAYER_MODELTYPE_RH_BOW_SLINGSHOT) ||
+           (sohModel == PLAYER_MODELTYPE_RH_BOW_SLINGSHOT_2) ||
+           (sohModel == PLAYER_MODELTYPE_RH_HOOKSHOT) || (sohModel == PLAYER_MODELTYPE_LH_BOOMERANG);
+}
